.TH BLUR_VIDEO.PY 1 "2026-09-25" "blur_video.py 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
blur_video.py \- obscure a rectangular region (or the whole frame) of one or more MP4 videos
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B blur_video.py
.RB ( \-F
.I mm
.RB "| " \-R " | " \-C )
.RB [ \-O
.IR ff ]
.RB [ \-A ]
.RB [ \-X
.I xx
.B \-Y
.IR yy
.RB [ \-DX
.I xx1
.B \-DY
.IR yy1 ]]
.RB [ \-S
.IR ss ]
.RB [ \-E
.IR ee ]
.RB [ \-M
.I mode
.IR gg ]
.RB [ \-D
.IR dd ]
.RB [ \-I
.IR ii ]
.RB [ \-T ]
.RB [ \-N ]
.br
.B blur_video.py
.RB \-H
.br
.B blur_video.py
.RB \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B blur_video.py
applies a privacy/censoring effect (Gaussian blur, pixelation, box blur,
median filter, or a solid color fill) to a rectangular region of one or
more MP4 video files, or to the entire frame, using
.BR ffmpeg (1)
as the underlying engine.
The effect can be restricted to a time window, feathered with a soft
fade at the edges, applied as several independent, chained passes read
from a parameter file, and previewed on a short test clip before
committing to a full run.
.PP
The script has no permanent external dependencies of its own: on first
run it checks for the Python packages
.B opencv-python
(used to read a video's width, height and duration) and
.B imageio-ffmpeg
(which bundles a portable
.B ffmpeg
binary, so no separate system installation of ffmpeg is required), and
installs them with
.B pip
automatically if they are missing. Dry-run mode
.RB ( \-N )
never opens or decodes a video and therefore does not need
.BR opencv-python .
.SH FILE SELECTION
Exactly one of the following three is required, and they are mutually
exclusive:
.TP
.BI \-F " mm"
Process a single input file, or a shell-style wildcard pattern, given as
.IR mm .
Quote patterns containing wildcards so the shell does not expand them
first, e.g.
.BR "\-F \(dqmovie*.mp4\(dq" .
.TP
.B \-R
Recursively process every
.B .mp4
file in the current directory and all subdirectories.
.TP
.B \-C
Process every
.B .mp4
file in the current directory only (not recursive).
.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-O " ff"
Write output files into folder
.IR ff ,
which is created if it does not already exist. If omitted, each output
file is written next to its corresponding input file.
.PP
Output filenames are the input filename with
.B _blurred
appended before the extension (e.g.
.IR movie.mp4 " \(-> " movie_blurred.mp4 ).
If a file of that name already exists, a two-digit numeric suffix
.RB ( _00 ", " _01 ", " "..." )
is appended until the name is unique; existing files are never
overwritten.
.SH REGION AND EFFECT OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-A
Apply the effect to the entire video frame instead of a rectangular
region. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-X / \-Y / \-DX / \-DY ,
.BR \-D ,
and
.BR \-I .
.TP
.BI \-X " xx"
X coordinate (pixels from the left) of the upper-left corner of the
region to affect. Required unless
.B \-A
or
.B \-I
is used.
.TP
.BI \-Y " yy"
Y coordinate (pixels from the top) of the upper-left corner of the
region. Required unless
.B \-A
or
.B \-I
is used.
.TP
.BI \-DX " xx1"
Width, in pixels, of the region. If omitted, the region extends from
.I xx
to the right edge of the frame. If
.IR xx " + " xx1
would extend past the right edge, the region is clamped to end at the
edge instead of failing.
.TP
.BI \-DY " yy1"
Height, in pixels, of the region, with the same "omit or clamp to the
bottom edge" behaviour as
.BR \-DX .
.PP
If
.I xx
or
.I yy
itself lies outside the frame's actual dimensions, that file is skipped
with an error (see
.B ERROR HANDLING
below); this can only be detected once the video has actually been
opened, so it does not apply in dry-run mode.
.TP
.BI \-M " mode gg"
Select the obscuring effect and its strength.
.I mode
is a single letter and
.I gg
is a strength from 0 to 100; the two are given as separate
arguments (e.g.
.BR "\-M P 60" ).
If
.B \-M
is omitted entirely, the default is
.B "G 50"
(medium Gaussian blur). The modes are:
.RS
.TP
.B G
\(en Gaussian blur.
.I gg
scales the blur radius; 100 flattens the region to a single average
colour.
.TP
.B P
\(en Pixelate / mosaic.
.I gg
controls block coarseness: low values give small, subtle blocks; 100
collapses the region to just a couple of large blocks.
.TP
.B B
\(en Box blur.
.I gg
scales the blur radius similarly to
.BR G ,
but with a blockier, harder-edged look and no risk of the region ever
appearing "softly" blurred instead of destroyed.
.TP
.B M
\(en Median filter.
.I gg
scales the smoothing radius. The radius is intentionally capped low
(regardless of how large
.I gg
or the region is) because this filter's cost grows quickly with radius.
.TP
.B S
\(en Solid fill.
.I gg
is not a "strength" but a fill
.B brightness
from 0 (black) to 100 (white), with 50 giving mid-grey. The fill is
always fully opaque no matter what value is given \(en there is no
"gg\ =\ 0 means no effect" case for this mode, unlike the other four.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-D " dd"
Feather the region's edge: grow the affected box by
.I dd
pixels on every side, with the effect's strength ramping
.B linearly
from full strength at the original box's edge down to zero at
.I dd
pixels beyond it, producing a soft transition instead of a hard
rectangular boundary. The ramp is built from a blurred mask using a box
blur, which is what makes the falloff exactly linear rather than the
smoother S-curve a Gaussian falloff would give.
Not usable with
.BR \-A ,
since a whole-frame effect has no edge to feather. When
.B \-I
is used,
.B \-D
on the command line is ignored; each line of the parameter file
supplies its own fade width instead (see
.B PARAMETER FILE
below).
.SH TIME WINDOW OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-S " ss"
Start time, in seconds, at which the effect begins. If omitted, the
effect starts at the beginning of the video (or, in test mode without
.BR \-I ,
determines where the 5-second test clip itself is extracted from).
.TP
.BI \-E " ee"
End time, in seconds, at which the effect stops. If omitted, the effect
continues to the end of the video.
.PP
If only
.B \-S
is given, the effect runs from
.I ss
to the end; if only
.B \-E
is given, it runs from the start to
.IR ee .
Values are silently clamped to the video's actual duration rather than
treated as an error.
.SH TEST MODE
.TP
.B \-T
Instead of processing the full video, extract a short (up to 5 second)
test clip so settings can be checked before committing to a full run.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B Without \-I:
two files are produced, the unmodified clip
.RI ( name _test-orig.mp4 )
and the same clip with the effect applied
.RI ( name _test_blurred.mp4 ),
both starting at
.B \-S
(or the beginning of the video if
.B \-S
is not given). The effect is applied across the whole clip regardless
of any
.B \-S / \-E
window, since the point of the test is to preview the region and
strength, not the timing.
.IP \(bu 2
.B With \-I:
one original clip is produced per parameter-file line
.RI ( name _test-orig_ NN .mp4),
each starting at
.B that line's own S
value (so the preview shows the actual moment in the video where that
pass is meant to apply), together with a matching blurred clip for that
pass alone
.RI ( name _test_blurred_ NN .mp4).
Passes are previewed independently, not chained together, so each can
be judged on its own.
.RE
.SH BATCH PARAMETER FILE
.TP
.BI \-I " ii"
Read blur parameters from the text file
.I ii
instead of from the command line. Every line's parameters are applied,
in order, to
.B every
matched input file, chained together into a single encode (i.e. one
output file per input, with all passes' effects baked in). This is
meant for applying several distinct blurred regions to the same file in
one pass.
.PP
When
.B \-I
is used,
.BR \-X , " \-Y" , " \-DX" , " \-DY" , " \-M" , " \-D" , " \-S " and " \-E"
on the command line are all ignored (a note is printed if any of them
were given); each line of the file supplies its own values instead.
.B \-A
cannot be combined with
.B \-I
at all, since the file format has no "whole frame" notion \(em to blur
an entire frame as one of several passes, specify a region covering the
full frame dimensions instead, and it will be clamped automatically if
it doesn't fit exactly.
.SS Parameter file format
Each non-blank line that does not start with
.B #
is one pass, with exactly eight semicolon-separated fields in this
order:
.PP
.RS
.B X;DX;Y;DY;M;D;S;E
.RE
.PP
.TP
.B X, Y
Upper-left corner of the region. Required, whole numbers, not negative.
.TP
.B DX, DY
Width and height of the region. May be left blank to mean "extend to
the frame edge", exactly like their command-line equivalents.
.TP
.B M
Mode letter immediately followed by the strength value, with
.B no
separator between them \(em e.g.
.BR G70 ", " P45 ", " B60 ", " M20 ", " S0 .
May be left blank, meaning
.B G50
(the same default as the command line).
.TP
.B D
Fade width in pixels for this pass, exactly as
.B \-D
above. May be left blank or given as
.B 0
for no feathering.
.TP
.B S, E
Start and end time in seconds for this pass. May be left blank, meaning
0 and end-of-video respectively.
.PP
Example file with two passes, the first a heavily pixelated region
starting 5 seconds in, the second a lightly feathered Gaussian blur
covering the whole clip:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ft CR
20;80;20;60;P80;5;5;
150;100;100;90;G40;10;;
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
The file is fully parsed and validated
.B before
any file is opened or any output is written; if any line is malformed,
the run aborts immediately with the specific line number and problem
described, rather than failing partway through a batch.
.SH DRY RUN
.TP
.B \-N
Show what would be done \(em which files were matched, what region,
mode, strength, fade and time window would apply to each, and what
output filename would be used \(em without opening, probing, or
modifying any file. Because no video is actually opened in this mode,
region and time bounds are
.B not
checked against the real video dimensions or duration; a region that
would be silently clamped or rejected in a real run may be reported
as-is here. Dry-run mode does not require
.B opencv-python
to be installed, since it never needs to read a video's properties.
.SH HELP
.TP
.B \-H
Show a brief usage summary and exit immediately. All other switches on
the command line are ignored when
.B \-H
is present, even if they would otherwise be invalid or incomplete.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the program name and version number (e.g.
.BR "blur_video.py 1.0.0" )
and exit immediately. Like
.BR \-H ,
all other switches are ignored when
.B \-V
is present.
.SH INTERRUPTING
The script can be stopped at any time with
.BR Ctrl-C .
The underlying
.B ffmpeg
process is terminated, and the output file that was being written at
the moment of interruption is deleted automatically and silently, so no
partial or corrupt file is left behind. Any output file that had
already finished successfully earlier in the same run is left in place.
The same automatic cleanup also applies if
.B ffmpeg
itself fails partway through for any other reason.
.SH ERROR HANDLING
Problems with an individual input file \(em it no longer exists, is
empty, cannot be read due to permissions, or is not actually a valid
video despite the
.B .mp4
name or extension \(em cause that file to be skipped, with the run
continuing on to the remaining files. The same applies to an output
location that cannot be created or is not writable (e.g. a read-only
folder given to
.BR \-O ).
.PP
Every skipped file is recorded in
.B blur_errors.log
in the current working directory, one line per problem, each with a
timestamp. This file is only created the moment the first actual error
occurs; a run in which every file succeeds leaves no log file behind.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B blur_errors.log
Created on demand (see
.B ERROR HANDLING
above) in the current working directory, listing any files that were
skipped and why.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All matched files were processed successfully (individual file failures
are logged but do not, by themselves, produce a non-zero exit status;
check the summary line and
.B blur_errors.log
to distinguish "0 failed" from "some failed").
.TP
.B 1
No input files matched the given
.BR \-F / \-R / \-C
selection, or a parameter file given to
.B \-I
could not be parsed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid combination of command-line switches (reported by the argument
parser itself before any file is touched).
.TP
.B 130
The run was interrupted with Ctrl-C.
.SH EXAMPLES
Blur a fixed region for an entire video with a medium Gaussian blur:
.PP
.RS
.nf
blur_video.py \-F movie.mp4 \-X 100 \-Y 50 \-DX 300 \-DY 200 \-M G 70
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pixelate the whole frame from 10s to 20s, recursively over subfolders,
writing results to
.BR ./out :
.PP
.RS
.nf
blur_video.py \-R \-A \-S 10 \-E 20 \-M P 90 \-O ./out
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview a soft-edged box blur on a 5-second test clip before committing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
blur_video.py \-F movie.mp4 \-X 100 \-Y 50 \-DX 300 \-DY 200 \e
              \-M B 70 \-D 12 \-T
.fi
.RE
.PP
Apply several different blurred regions to every file in the current
folder, using a parameter file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
blur_video.py \-C \-I passes.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check what a batch run would do without touching any files:
.PP
.RS
.nf
blur_video.py \-R \-I passes.txt \-N
.fi
.RE
.SH CAVEATS AND NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
.B No hard numeric limits are pre-validated for region geometry beyond
.B basic sanity.
.I xx
or
.I yy
lying outside the frame aborts that file with an error; this can only
be detected once the file is actually opened, so it is invisible in
dry-run mode.
.IP \(bu 2
.B The median filter's radius is capped low
(regardless of the requested strength or region size) because its
computational cost rises quickly with radius; very large regions at
high
.B M
strength will not get a proportionally larger radius the way
.B G
or
.B B
would.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "Box blur's radius limit differs between the luma and chroma planes" .
ffmpeg imposes separate maximum radii for each, and the chroma limit is
roughly half the luma one under standard 4:2:0 chroma subsampling
(which essentially all normal MP4 sources use). The script computes and
clamps both independently, so this is handled correctly, but it does
mean a very large requested box-blur strength on a very small region
may be silently capped below what the strength value alone would
suggest.
.IP \(bu 2
.B The fade (\-D) transition is linear, not smoothly curved.
This is a deliberate design choice (achieved via a box-blurred mask)
rather than a limitation of the effect filters themselves, since a
box blur applied to a hard edge produces an exactly linear ramp, unlike
the S-curve a Gaussian-blurred mask would give.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Feathering (\-D) adds modest extra processing cost,
since the affected region is cropped slightly larger and blended with a
generated soft mask rather than simply overlaid. In testing this added
only a few percent to total run time for typical region sizes; very
large fade widths relative to the video's own dimensions would add
proportionally more.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Solid fill (\-M S) never has a "strength 0 means no effect" case,
unlike the other four modes; a value of 0 still produces a fully
opaque black fill, not an untouched region.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Test-mode clips are always re-encoded, not stream-copied,
even the unmodified "_test-orig" clip, so that trimming to an exact
sub-second start time is frame-accurate. This makes test-clip
extraction slightly slower than a simple cut would be, but avoids
inaccurate trim points that can occur when cutting compressed video on
non-keyframe boundaries.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Audio is always preserved via stream copy,
never re-encoded, for both the main output and test clips (except the
test clip's own audio track, which is re-encoded to AAC alongside its
frame-accurate video re-encode).
.IP \(bu 2
.B File discovery for \-R and \-C only matches the
.B .mp4
extension, case-insensitively; other container formats (
.BR .mov ,
.B .mkv
etc.) are not matched even though ffmpeg itself could process them.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Only one blur pass per line is supported in the parameter file;
overlapping regions across different lines are blended in file order
(later lines drawn on top of earlier ones), not merged or averaged.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Not applicable \(em generated for local use.
